{"name":"Somnambulist","hex":"#778899","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"somnambulist","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A person who walks about in their sleep; a sleepwalker."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/somnambulist"}],"entries":[{"title":"Somnambulist (Simply Being Loved)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Somnambulist (Simply Being Loved)\" is a song by American musician BT, which contains vocals from American singer JC Chasez. It was released through Nettwerk in August 2003, as the lead single from BT's fourth studio album, Emotional Technology (2003). The techno-pop song was written and produced by BT. It consists of a bass drum, snare drum and Roland JP-8000, and contains 6,178 stutter edits. The lyrics were influenced by American writer Henry Miller and use a recurring mantra."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/18/BT_-_Somnambulist.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somnambulist_(Simply_Being_Loved)"},{"title":"Somnambulist (Angel)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Somnambulist\" is the 11th episode of the first season of the American television series Angel."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somnambulist_(Angel)"},{"title":"Sleepwalking","blocks":[{"text":"Sleepwalking, also known as somnambulism or noctambulism, is a phenomenon of combined sleep and wakefulness. It is classified as a sleep disorder belonging to the parasomnia family. It can also be understood as a disorder of state dissociation, which features of wakefulness and deep non-REM sleep occurring simultaneously in different brain regions. It occurs during the slow wave stage of sleep, in a state of low consciousness, with performance of activities that are usually performed during a state of full consciousness. These activities can be as benign as talking, sitting up in bed, walking to a bathroom, consuming food, and cleaning, or as hazardous as cooking, driving a motor vehicle, violent gestures and grabbing at hallucinated objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Kramskoi_somnambule.jpg/330px-Kramskoi_somnambule.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sleepwalking"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Sonnambula_atto_3.jpg/250px-Sonnambula_atto_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8e/Sonnambula_atto_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Sonnambula_atto_3.jpg/250px-Sonnambula_atto_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Amina, the somnabuliste, at the mill","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASonnambula_atto_3.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg/500px-Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg/1280px-Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg/500px-Mary_Hoare_-_Lady_Macbeth%2C_Sleepwalking_-_B1975.4.1972_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mary Hoare's painting Lady Macbeth, 